{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>On the second day, etc.</b> The tribe of Issachar was given precedence. Because it was a tribe of Torah, it was elevated and preceded Reuben the firstborn. And not only that, but Zebulun as well, who supported Issachar's hand to study Torah, as it is written (Deuteronomy 33:18): ״שְׂמַח זְבוּלֻן בְּצֵאתֶךָ וְיִשָּׂשכָר״ etc. — he sat in his shade and preceded Reuben." "<b>He offered Nethanel, etc., prince of Issachar.</b> He gave precedence to the mention of his name over the mention of his princely office, to establish for him a framework of distinction even apart from the importance of the princehood — unlike all the other princes, for whom the mention of the princehood preceded their names. And in his name there is an allusion to the remembrance of Torah and the means of its acquisition: the remembrance of Torah — <b>Nethanel</b>: נָתַן אֵל, after the verse (Proverbs 4:2): ״כִּי לֶקַח טוֹב נָתַתִּי לָכֶם״. The means of its acquisition — <b>ben Tzuar</b>: for Torah is acquired only through suffering, as is its way (Gitin 67a): ״אָדָם כִּי יָמוּת בְּאֹהֶל״. And perhaps it is for this reason as well that he gave precedence to the mention of his name, to say that it was by virtue of the quality inscribed in his name that he merited to become prince over the children of Issachar, those who know understanding of the times."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:06:37.027352120Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}